  "slug": "auxin-topic-eb88",
  "summary": "Recent preprints resolve where auxin perception, expansin delivery and vascular patterning are spatially restricted in roots and petals.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is spatial restriction of developmental signals in roots. A synthetic auxin-perception system in defined Arabidopsis root domains shows that outer elongating cells control root growth, and shifting the domain moves the proliferation zone. ROP activation recruits the trichoblast-specific expansin EXPA7 to root hair initiation sites via brefeldin-A-sensitive vesicle trafficking. Metaxylem number is remodelled along wheat and grass root axes, with spatial transcriptomics and hydraulic modelling linking vessel patterning to water transport. An Arabidopsis study on nuclear and chromatin geometry cautions against direct interpretation of CYCB1;1 reporter intensity. In Hibiscus trionum petals, auxin does not initiate bullseye boundaries but preserves their proportions through distal cell behaviour. What is new is the separation of initiation from proportion and expansion, with the petal case being the only non-root example.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
